WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:06.114
Hi! Today, let's learn how to find the area of a triangle.

2
00:00:06.264 --> 00:00:12.874
In the garden, there is a rectangular flower bed. It is 8 metres long and 6 metres wide.

3
00:00:13.024 --> 00:00:22.250
Each small square is 1 square metre. There are 8 times 6, which is 48 squares. So the area is 48 square metres.

4
00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:29.538
The gardener cuts it along a diagonal into two triangle beds: one for red flowers, and one for yellow.

5
00:00:29.688 --> 00:00:33.306
So, how big is one triangle bed?

6
00:00:33.456 --> 00:00:41.314
Pick up the red triangle and turn it half a turn. It fits exactly on top of the yellow one. They are the same size!

7
00:00:41.464 --> 00:00:51.034
So each triangle is half of the rectangle. Half of 48 is 24. One triangle bed is 24 square metres.

8
00:00:51.184 --> 00:00:55.394
But what if the triangle does not have a right angle, like this one?

9
00:00:55.544 --> 00:01:02.778
Let's draw a rectangle around it. Its length is the base of the triangle, and its width is the height of the triangle.

10
00:01:02.928 --> 00:01:10.306
From the top corner, draw a line straight down to the base, at a right angle. Now we have two smaller rectangles.

11
00:01:10.456 --> 00:01:18.962
Look! In each small rectangle, the triangle takes up exactly half. A is the same as A, and B is the same as B.

12
00:01:19.112 --> 00:01:23.082
So the whole triangle is also half of the big rectangle.

13
00:01:23.232 --> 00:01:30.834
That gives us the formula. Area of triangle equals one half, times base, times height.

14
00:01:30.984 --> 00:01:38.770
The most important part is the height. It must be perpendicular to the base. That means they meet at a right angle.

15
00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:43.802
The height is the perpendicular distance from the base to the opposite corner.

16
00:01:43.952 --> 00:01:51.066
Sometimes the height is outside the triangle. Then we extend the base with a dotted line, and draw the height to it.

17
00:01:51.216 --> 00:01:58.650
Whether the height is inside or outside, the formula is the same: one half times base times height.

18
00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:09.298
Let's try one. This triangle has a base of 10 centimetres, a height of 7 centimetres, and a slanted side of 8 centimetres. What is its area?

19
00:02:09.448 --> 00:02:17.810
Step 1. Find the base, and the height that is perpendicular to it: base 10 centimetres, height 7 centimetres.

20
00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:23.418
Step 2. Use the formula: one half times 10 times 7.

21
00:02:23.568 --> 00:02:28.378
10 times 7 is 70. Half of 70 is 35.

22
00:02:28.528 --> 00:02:36.082
So the area is 35 square centimetres. Don't forget the unit: square centimetres.

23
00:02:36.232 --> 00:02:45.026
Watch out! Some pupils use the slanted side, and work out one half times 10 times 8, which is 40. That is wrong!

24
00:02:45.176 --> 00:02:53.442
The slanted side is not at a right angle to the base, so it is not the height. The answer is 35 square centimetres.

25
00:02:53.592 --> 00:03:02.586
Before you work it out, ask yourself: is this line perpendicular to the base? And remember to multiply by one half!

26
00:03:02.736 --> 00:03:14.818
Your turn! This triangle has a base of 12 centimetres and a height of 5 centimetres. What is its area? Pause and think.

27
00:03:14.968 --> 00:03:22.714
The answer is one half times 12 times 5, which is 30 square centimetres. Did you get it right?

28
00:03:22.864 --> 00:03:31.130
Remember: the area of a triangle equals one half times base times height, and the height must be perpendicular to the base.

29
00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:36.218
Now go and practise in your garden, and grow more flowers!

